Subject: ntdll: resolve the D3D/Vulkan builtins ahead of the registry load order
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This tree ships its D3D/Vulkan stack as Wine builtins: DXVK provides d3d9/d3d8
(and d3d11/d3d10core/dxgi on FreeBSD), DXMT provides d3d11/d3d10core/dxgi on
macOS, and winevulkan provides vulkan-1 (backed by KosmicKrisp on macOS).
wined3d's D3D9/D3D8 is broken here, and an app-bundled native loader (e.g. Steam
CEF's own vulkan-1.dll) exposes no WSI, so each of these must resolve to builtin.

The reason is the *registry*, and only the registry. A bottle's DllOverrides are
persistent state written by things this tree does not control: an older Whisky
that installed DXVK per-game as native, winetricks, a game's own installer. Such
an entry does not select a different provider — the builtins we ship carry the
"Wine builtin DLL" signature, so a leftover "d3d9"="native" matches nothing at
all and the app dies on STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND with a usable d3d9.dll sitting in
system32, syswow64 and its own directory. That was a real failure: Super Street
Fighter IV, exit c0000135, traced to a single stale value.

So extend get_load_order() — the same place Proton already hard-codes the
EasyAntiCheat bridge — to resolve these basenames to builtin before the
DllOverrides keys are consulted.

WINEDLLOVERRIDES still wins, in all three spellings get_load_order_value() would
have tried: the full module name, "*basename", and basename. It is set per run
so it cannot go stale, and it is the lever for A/B-ing a provider (wined3d vs
DXMT, say) during debugging. Checking only the bare name — as an earlier version
of this did — silently ignored "*d3d9=n", the form winetricks and most of the
documentation use, while the TRACE still reported "fork builtin"; an A/B run
then reads as "the override made no difference to the bug".

Note what this deliberately does *not* claim. An earlier version of this message
argued that a host-set WINEDLLOVERRIDES could not catch a full-path
app-directory load, which would have made the env route unusable and this patch
the only option. That is wrong: the "*basename" rung a few lines below runs
unconditionally, including for loads by explicit path, so a per-bottle
WINEDLLOVERRIDES would catch Steam CEF's bundled vulkan-1.dll perfectly well.
The env route is viable and simpler; it is the *stale registry* that an env var
cannot defend against, since the registry is consulted whether or not anyone set
an env.

Two consequences worth stating. Wine's own per-application overrides for these
modules — loader/wine.inf.in ships rayne1/rayne2 d3d8=native and RDR2
vulkan-1=native — are inert in this tree, since AppDefaults is part of the
registry this now resolves ahead of. And the module set lives in several places
(here, the build scripts, tests/); nothing enforces agreement.

The D3D/DXGI/vulkan-1 modules apply on every host this tree targets (macOS and
FreeBSD) — the provider differs but "builtin" is always right — so they are
unguarded. Only winemetal (DXMT's Metal interface) stays behind __APPLE__.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c
index 722190da255..45d7a3f7466 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loadorder.c
@@ -396,6 +396,29 @@ enum loadorder get_load_order( const UNICODE_STRING *nt_name )
     static const WCHAR easyanticheat_x64W[] = {'e','a','s','y','a','n','t','i','c','h','e','a','t','_','x','6','4',0};
     static const WCHAR easyanticheatW[] = {'e','a','s','y','a','n','t','i','c','h','e','a','t',0};
     static const WCHAR soW[] = {'.','s','o',0};
+    /* D3D and Vulkan are shipped as Wine builtins on every host this tree targets:
+     * macOS uses DXMT (d3d11/d3d10core/dxgi) + DXVK (d3d9/d3d8), FreeBSD uses DXVK
+     * for all of them; vulkan-1 is winevulkan. wined3d's D3D9 is broken and an
+     * app-bundled native loader (e.g. Steam CEF's vulkan-1.dll) exposes no WSI, so
+     * all of these must resolve to builtin — the provider differs by platform, but
+     * "builtin" is always right. winemetal is DXMT's Metal interface DLL, so it is
+     * the one entry that is macOS-only. */
+    static const WCHAR d3d9W[]      = {'d','3','d','9',0};
+    static const WCHAR d3d8W[]      = {'d','3','d','8',0};
+    static const WCHAR d3d11W[]     = {'d','3','d','1','1',0};
+    static const WCHAR d3d10coreW[] = {'d','3','d','1','0','c','o','r','e',0};
+    static const WCHAR dxgiW[]      = {'d','x','g','i',0};
+    static const WCHAR vulkan1W[]   = {'v','u','l','k','a','n','-','1',0};
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    static const WCHAR winemetalW[] = {'w','i','n','e','m','e','t','a','l',0};
+#endif
+    static const WCHAR * const builtin_onlyW[] =
+    {
+        d3d9W, d3d8W, d3d11W, d3d10coreW, dxgiW, vulkan1W,
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+        winemetalW,
+#endif
+    };
 
     static const WCHAR prefixW[] = {'\\','?','?','\\'};
     enum loadorder ret = LO_INVALID;
@@ -479,6 +502,43 @@ enum loadorder get_load_order( const UNICODE_STRING *nt_name )
         }
     }
 
+    /* The fork's D3D/Vulkan builtins, resolved before any registry lookup. A
+     * bottle's DllOverrides outlive whatever wrote them — an old Whisky that
+     * dropped DXVK into a game directory as *native*, winetricks, a game's own
+     * installer — and this tree ships those same modules as builtins, carrying
+     * the "Wine builtin DLL" signature. A stale "d3d9"="native" therefore does
+     * not select a different provider, it makes every candidate unloadable and
+     * the app dies on STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND with the DLL sitting right there.
+     * WINEDLLOVERRIDES still wins: it is set per run, so it cannot go stale, and
+     * it is the lever for A/B-ing a provider (e.g. wined3d vs DXMT). */
+    for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(builtin_onlyW); i++)
+        if (!wcsicmp( basename, builtin_onlyW[i] )) break;
+
+    if (i < ARRAY_SIZE(builtin_onlyW))
+    {
+        /* Every WINEDLLOVERRIDES spelling get_load_order_value() would have
+         * tried, in its order: the full module name, "*basename", basename.
+         * add_load_order_set() stores each key verbatim and get_env_load_order()
+         * matches it whole, so checking only the bare name silently ignored
+         * "*d3d9=n" — the form winetricks and most of the documentation use.
+         * Silently is the problem: the TRACE below still said "fork builtin", so
+         * an A/B run reads as "the override made no difference to the bug". */
+        if ((ret = get_env_load_order( module+1 )) == LO_INVALID)
+        {
+            basename[-1] = '*';
+            if ((ret = get_env_load_order( basename-1 )) == LO_INVALID)
+                ret = get_env_load_order( basename );
+        }
+        if (ret != LO_INVALID)
+            TRACE( "got environment %s for %s\n", debugstr_loadorder(ret), debugstr_us(nt_name) );
+        else
+        {
+            ret = LO_BUILTIN;
+            TRACE( "got fork builtin %s for %s\n", debugstr_loadorder(ret), debugstr_us(nt_name) );
+        }
+        goto done;
+    }
+
     /* first explicit module name */
     if ((ret = get_load_order_value( std_key, app_key, module+1 )) != LO_INVALID)
         goto done;
